sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch10-24> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Essentially all methods of perception and analysis in practical use have similar capabilities, recognizing repetition and sometimes nesting but almost nothing more complex, possibly because only such regularities are both common and quickly recognizable, or because humans choose methods matching their built-in perception.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Practical perception and analysis methods recognize only repetition and nesting";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 10, “Processes of Perception and Analysis”; section “Higher Forms of Perception and Analysis” (from p. 632), p. 633. Scope: human perception and standard methods of mathematical, statistical and computational analysis"
}
